<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Milton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1608">1608</a>–<a href="/years/1674">1674</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Milton was an English poet, polemicist and civil servant, born in Bread Street, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and educated at Christ's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. His Areopagitica (1644) argued against pre-publication censorship, and after the Civil War he defended the regicide in The Tenure of Kings and Magistrates and Eikonoklastes (1649) while serving the Commonwealth's Council of State as Secretary for Foreign Tongues. By 1652 he was totally blind and dictated his work to amanuenses, among them Andrew Marvell. His epic Paradise Lost (1667), in blank verse, tells of the fall of man. After the Restoration of 1660 he lost his public platform, and he died impoverished in 1674.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-milton.jpg" alt="John Milton" width="330" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John-milton.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Portuguese accounts made Kilwa’s name known in Europe, and <b>John Milton</b>’s Paradise Lost (1667) lists ‘Quiloa’ beside Mombasa, Malindi and Sofala.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Milton</b> supported Cromwell and wrote Paradise Lost, and John Bunyan wrote The Pilgrim's Progress in 1678.</blockquote><a href="/movement/puritans">Puritans · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The Levellers, a minority movement mainly of Puritans, Presbyterians and Quakers, called for freedom of religion, frequent parliaments and equality under the law, and <b>John Milton</b>’s Areopagitica made one of the first arguments for freedom of speech.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In England James Harrington and <b>John Milton</b> were leading republican thinkers of the Commonwealth, and in the early 18th century the “country” party of the commonwealthmen denounced the corruption of the court, a theory that heavily influenced the American colonists.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Milton</b> issued Areopagitica, an unlicensed pamphlet arguing against Parliament's censorship of printing.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-23">23 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…as good almost kill a Man as kill a good Book; who kills a Man kills a reasonable creature, Gods Image; but hee who destroyes a good Booke, kills reason it selfe…&#8221;</p><footer>Areopagitica (1644) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Areopagitica_(1644)" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I cannot praise a fugitive and cloister'd vertue, unexercis'd &amp; unbreath'd, that never sallies out and sees her adversary, but slinks out of the race, where that immortall garland is to be run for, not without dust and heat.&#8221;</p><footer>Areopagitica (1644) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Areopagitica_(1644)" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Give me the liberty to know, to utter, and to argue freely according to conscience, above all liberties.&#8221;</p><footer>Areopagitica (1644) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Areopagitica_(1644)" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let her and Falshood grapple; who ever knew Truth put to the wors, in a free and open encounter.&#8221;</p><footer>Areopagitica (1644) &#183; original &#183; of Truth &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Areopagitica_(1644)" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
